$h1 How to use

$h2 Regular Java application

As a regular Java application, you can also use jsystemd.

First, import the `jsystemd-core` module as a dependency:

```xml
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd</groupId>
      <artifactId>jsystemd-core</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
```

$h3 Java 22 and later

On Java 22 and later, such as on the Java 25 LTS, jsystemd notifies systemd with the Foreign Function and Memory API of the JDK, rather than through
the [JNA](https://github.com/java-native-access/jna) library and libsystemd (see [Native library](native-library.html)). JNA is then no longer needed,
and can be left out of the dependencies of the application:

```xml
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd</groupId>
      <artifactId>jsystemd-core</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
      <exclusions>
        <exclusion>
          <groupId>net.java.dev.jna</groupId>
          <artifactId>jna</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
      </exclusions>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
```

The same exclusion applies to the other modules, such as `jsystemd-spring-boot-starter`. Keep JNA when the application may also run on Java 17 to 21,
or when systemd notifies it through a `vsock:` address, as from a virtual machine: without it, the integration is then disabled, with a warning (see
[Native library](native-library.html)).

Calling native code is a restricted operation from Java 22 on, which the JVM warns about unless the application allows it. Allow it by passing
`--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED` to `java`, or for an executable JAR, by adding `Enable-Native-Access: ALL-UNNAMED` to its manifest, as described in
[Native library](native-library.html#Enabling_native_access).

$h3 Integration

Then, integrate an instance of [Systemd](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) with the lifecycle of your application, as described below.
When the application is not running under systemd, the notifications are simply not sent, so the same code can run anywhere.

$h3 Lifecycle

The application is expected to go through the following steps, in this order:

1. **Get the [Systemd](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) instance**, as early as possible, with [Systemd.instance()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html).
   There is a single instance per process, as systemd follows a service through a single process.
   The instance is enough to notify systemd when the application is ready and when it is stopping. It can also enable some optional periodic tasks,
   depending on what the application needs, which can be enabled again with another period, or disabled, at any time:
    * [enableStatusUpdate(period)](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) sends the status to systemd periodically, for the status to stay current while the application runs;
    * [enableWatchdog()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) sends the watchdog heartbeat periodically, once the application is ready, when systemd expects it, that is, when the service unit sets `WatchdogSec`. systemd then
      sets the `WATCHDOG_USEC` environment variable, and the heartbeat is sent at half that interval, as systemd recommends. When the variable is not
      set, nothing is sent, so it is safe to always call it. A `WATCHDOG_USEC` of 1 cannot be honored, and throws an `IllegalStateException`. [enableWatchdog(period)](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) sends the heartbeat at a given period instead;
    * [enableExtendTimeout(period, amount)](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) extends the startup timeout (`TimeoutStartSec`) periodically, by `amount` each time, for
      applications that may take longer to start than that timeout allows. It stops once the application is ready or stopping, and can be enabled again
      once ready, or once stopping, to extend the shutdown timeout (`TimeoutStopSec`) instead (see step 6). It requires systemd 236 or later.
2. **Optionally, register status providers**, with [addStatusProviders(...)](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html), to compose the status shown by `systemctl status`: any of the built-in ones
   (such as [HeapStatusProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/HeapStatusProvider.html), [NonHeapStatusProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/NonHeapStatusProvider.html), [ClassLoaderStatusProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/ClassLoaderStatusProvider.html) and [ThreadStatusProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/ThreadStatusProvider.html)), your own implementations of
   [StatusProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/StatusProvider.html), or none at all: without status providers, no status is sent.
3. **Optionally, add health providers**, with [addHealthProviders(...)](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html): while any of them reports the application as unhealthy, the watchdog heartbeat is
   not sent, so that systemd restarts the application once `WatchdogSec` elapses. Use the built-in health providers: the [ThreadStatusProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/ThreadStatusProvider.html), which
   can report the application as unhealthy while any of its threads are deadlocked, when created with `new ThreadStatusProvider(true, true)`, and the
   [FileDescriptorStatusProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/FileDescriptorStatusProvider.html), which can report it as unhealthy when its file descriptors are about to run out, when created with
   `new FileDescriptorStatusProvider(true, 0.9)`; your own implementations of
   [HealthProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/HealthProvider.html); or both. Health providers whose unhealthy state may resolve by itself, such
   as those depending on external systems, can extend
   [DelayableHealthProvider](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/DelayableHealthProvider.html) instead, so that their unhealthy state is only reported once it
   lasts longer than the delay set with [setUnhealthyDelay(...)](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/DelayableHealthProvider.html), avoiding restarts on short outages.
   Health providers can also be removed, with [removeHealthProviders(...)](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html). Without health providers, the heartbeat only shows that the application is alive.
   See [Health providers](health-providers.html) for more information.
4. **Start the application.** Optionally, during a long startup, the status can be updated with [updateStatus()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html), and the startup timeout extended
   with [extendTimeout()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html), at each milestone, whether or not the periodic tasks are enabled.
5. **Call [ready()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html)** once the application is ready to do work. This tells systemd that the service has started, which a service with `Type=notify` needs:
   otherwise, systemd considers the startup failed once `TimeoutStartSec` elapses. The startup timeout is no longer extended afterwards, and its periodic
   extension, if enabled, stops.
6. **On shutdown, call [stopping()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) first**, before stopping the application itself. This tells systemd that the service is stopping, and also stops the
   watchdog heartbeats: from then on, systemd only enforces `TimeoutStopSec`, so the application can take the time it needs to stop. It can also be called
   before [ready()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html), when the application stops while it starts up: the periodic extension of the startup timeout then stops, and
   [ready()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) does nothing afterwards. A long shutdown can extend `TimeoutStopSec` the same way as a long startup extends
   `TimeoutStartSec`: with [extendTimeout()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) at each milestone, or with
   [enableExtendTimeout(period, amount)](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html), enabled again once ready, in preparation for the shutdown, or once stopping, as the
   periodic extension of the startup does not carry over to the shutdown, where it would keep a hung shutdown from ever timing out. Enabled once ready,
   it only starts extending the timeout once the application is stopping.
7. **Call [close()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) last**, once the application has stopped. This stops the periodic tasks. As the watchdog heartbeats stop with them, it also calls
   [stopping()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) first, if the application did not, so that systemd does not kill the application if the rest of its shutdown takes longer than
   `WatchdogSec`. Calling [stopping()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) as soon as the shutdown starts is still recommended: until then, systemd keeps enforcing the watchdog, and
   the shutdown may stop the heartbeats on its own, for instance when a health provider reports the application as unhealthy once a resource it checks is
   closed. A closed instance cannot be reused, but notifications sent directly to systemd, such as the status updates, keep working.

The shutdown steps belong wherever the application shuts down. `systemctl stop` stops the application with `SIGTERM`, which runs the JVM shutdown hooks,
so a shutdown hook is the most direct place for them, as in the example below. Applications built on a framework or container that has its own lifecycle
can instead hook into that lifecycle, as the Spring Boot starter does with the Spring application context. Either way, add `SuccessExitStatus=143` to the
service unit (see below), so that systemd does not consider an exit caused by `SIGTERM` to be a failure.

$h3 Example

The following example uses every feature, including the optional ones (the periodic tasks, the status providers and the health providers). Only
[ready()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html), [stopping()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) and [close()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) are needed for a minimal integration.

```java
import com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd.ClassLoaderStatusProvider;
import com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd.HealthProvider.Health;
import com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd.HeapStatusProvider;
import com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd.NonHeapStatusProvider;
import com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd.Systemd;
import com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd.ThreadStatusProvider;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Map;

public class MyApplication {

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Systemd systemd = Systemd.instance();
    // the periodic tasks are all optional: the instance alone is enough for a minimal integration
    systemd.enableStatusUpdate(Duration.ofSeconds(5));
    // only sends heartbeats when systemd expects them, that is, when the service unit sets WatchdogSec
    systemd.enableWatchdog();
    systemd.enableExtendTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5), Duration.ofSeconds(29));
    // detects deadlocked threads, showing them in the status, and reports them as unhealthy
    ThreadStatusProvider threads = new ThreadStatusProvider(true, true);
    systemd.addStatusProviders(new HeapStatusProvider(), new NonHeapStatusProvider(), new ClassLoaderStatusProvider(), threads);
    systemd.logStatus();

    MyService service = new MyService();
    systemd.addHealthProviders(threads,
        () -> service.isHealthy() ? Health.HEALTHY : new Health(false, Map.of("reason", service.problem())));

    Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
      systemd.stopping();
      service.stop();
      try {
        systemd.close();
      } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
      }
    }));

    service.start();
    systemd.ready();
  }
}
```

$h2 Under Spring Boot

When using Spring Boot, simply import the `jsystemd-spring-boot-starter` module as a dependency:

```xml
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd</groupId>
      <artifactId>jsystemd-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
```

Autoconfiguration takes place through the class [SystemdAutoConfiguration](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/spring/autoconfigure/SystemdAutoConfiguration.html),
and will notify systemd once your application starts up successfully, when it becomes ready to accept traffic
([ReadinessState.ACCEPTING_TRAFFIC](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/api/java/org/springframework/boot/availability/ReadinessState.html)).
Everything described above for regular Java applications is then taken care of automatically.

An application only accepts traffic once its `ApplicationRunner` and `CommandLineRunner` beans have run, so a runner that runs for long, or never ends,
such as one that processes a queue, keeps the service starting up, until the startup times out. Either:

* set `systemd.ready-event=application-started`, to notify systemd once the application has started, as soon as its application context is refreshed,
  before the runners run; or
* call [Systemd.instance().ready()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) from the runner, once it is ready to do work, such as once it has
  connected to the queue.

systemd is only notified once, so the starter notifying it again once the application accepts traffic does nothing.

$h3 Manual wiring

To wire the integration yourself instead, depend on the `jsystemd-spring-boot` module, which has the building blocks of the starter without its
autoconfiguration:

```xml
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd</groupId>
      <artifactId>jsystemd-spring-boot</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
```

Nothing is then set up automatically: the application takes the [Systemd](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) instance through the
[lifecycle](#Lifecycle) described above for regular Java applications. The following example only covers the basics of that lifecycle, with the watchdog
enabled, leaving out the other periodic tasks and the status and health providers:

```java
import com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd.Systemd;
import com.github.jpmsilva.jsystemd.spring.SystemdLifecycle;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;

@SpringBootApplication
public class MyApplication {

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Systemd systemd = Systemd.instance();
    // only sends heartbeats when systemd expects them, that is, when the service unit sets WatchdogSec
    systemd.enableWatchdog();
    SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);
    systemd.ready();
  }

  // tells systemd that the application is stopping, as soon as the application context starts closing
  @Bean
  SystemdLifecycle systemdLifecycle(ApplicationContext context) {
    return new SystemdLifecycle(context);
  }

  // closes the instance, which stops the watchdog heartbeats, when the application context is closed
  @Bean(destroyMethod = "close")
  Systemd systemd() {
    return Systemd.instance();
  }
}
```

systemd is told that the application is ready once `SpringApplication.run(...)` returns, that is, once the application has started and its runners have
run. As with the starter, a runner that runs for long, or never ends, keeps the service starting up, until the startup times out: call
[ready()](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/Systemd.html) earlier instead, from the runner, once it is ready to do work, or from a listener of
[ApplicationStartedEvent](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/api/java/org/springframework/boot/context/event/ApplicationStartedEvent.html), which is
published before the runners run.

[SystemdLifecycle](apidocs/com/github/jpmsilva/jsystemd/spring/SystemdLifecycle.html) tells systemd that the application is stopping before the beans of
the application context are stopped, such as the embedded web server.

$h2 Systemd service unit

When using this library, service units should now use `Type=notify` under the `[Service]` unit configuration:

```
[Unit]
Description=MyService
Requires=network.target
After=network.target
After=syslog.target

[Service]
Type=notify
WorkingDirectory=/opt/myservice
ExecStart=/opt/jdk17/bin/java -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError -jar /opt/myservice/myservice.jar
SuccessExitStatus=143
KillMode=mixed
TimeoutStopSec=10
TimeoutStartSec=30

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

In the above example, `/opt/myservice/myservice.jar` is a packed Java application. On Java 22 and later, also pass
`--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED` to `java` in `ExecStart`, unless the manifest of the JAR declares `Enable-Native-Access: ALL-UNNAMED`, so that the
JVM does not warn that jsystemd calls native code (see [Native library](native-library.html#Enabling_native_access)):

```
ExecStart=/opt/jdk25/bin/java --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError -jar /opt/myservice/myservice.jar
```
